---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "migration", label: "Migration" },
  { id: "when", label: "Choose the replacement" },
  { id: "semantics", label: "Accessibility and behavior" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="banners">
  <PageBody sections={S}>
    <Section {...S.migration}>
      <p>The banner component has been removed. This page remains available for existing links.</p>
      <p>
        Remove <code>.banner</code>, its <code>rich</code>, <code>vibrant</code> and
        <code>square</code> modifiers, and <code>--md-comp-banners-*</code> overrides
        from former banner markup. Those modifiers remain valid on other components
        that document them. Custom Sass builds must also remove the <code>banners</code> partial.
      </p>
      <p>
        Banners were a framework pattern related to
        <a href="https://m2.material.io/components/banners">legacy Material 2 banners</a>.
        The reviewed current Material 3 inventory has no dedicated banner entry;
        this is not evidence that Google explicitly deprecated them.
      </p>
    </Section>
    <Section {...S.when}>
      <ul>
        <li>Persistent, nonblocking issues: native inline text and controls near the affected content.</li>
        <li>Temporary feedback after an action: <a href={route("snackbar")}>snackbar</a>.</li>
        <li>Decisions that must block progress: <a href={route("dialogs")}>dialog</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>An offline message can remain in normal document flow while editing continues:</p>
      <Code code={`
<div>
  <p>You're offline. Edits are saved locally and will sync later.</p>
  <button class="button text" type="button">Retry sync</button>
</div>
`} />
      <p>Connect the button to your application's retry action. No new component or initialization is needed.</p>
    </Section>
    <Section {...S.semantics}>
      <p>
        Static messages need no live region. For changes during use, create an empty
        <code>role="status"</code> text node before updating its text. Keep action
        buttons outside the live region. Do not move focus just to announce a status.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use named buttons for commands, not a navigation landmark. The
        <code>role="banner"</code> landmark identifies the page header, not a message.
        If your application allows dismissal, restore logical focus when removing
        the focused control. Allow text and actions to wrap; check narrow screens
        and enlarged text without fixed heights or clipped controls.
      </p>
    </Section>
  </PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
